<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Deli Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1632 CE – 1946 CE (approx.) · capital Medan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East Sumatran Malay sultanate whose land concessions created the famous Deli tobacco industry and the city of Medan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Deli was founded in 1632 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuanku-gocah-pahlawan">Tuanku Gocah Pahlawan</a>, a commander sent by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> to secure the coast of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. His descendants ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/siak-sultanate">Siak Sultanate</a>, before Deli declared itself an independent sultanate in the 19th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans governed Malay coastal communities and held authority over neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karo-batak">Karo Batak</a> lands. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took control in 1862, the court leased vast tracts to planters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-nienhuys">Jacob Nienhuys</a>, whose wrapper tobacco became world famous. Chinese and Javanese contract labourers were brought in, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamun-al-rasyid-perkasa-alam">Ma'mun Al Rasyid Perkasa Alam</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimun-palace">Maimun Palace</a> in Medan in 1888.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Deli became the wealthiest of the self-governing states of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and Medan grew into a major city. The social revolution of March 1946 ended the sultanate as a political power, though the sultan remains a ceremonial figure in Medan today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/deli-sultanate.jpg" alt="Deli Sultanate" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sultanate_of_Deli.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1632 CE – 1946 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Medan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tuanku Gocah Pahlawan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Deli, Deli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1789&amp;focus=deli-sultanate" aria-label="Deli Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 2043 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Deli Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7738 2299L7642 2226M7738 2299L7959 2663M7738 2299L7959 2664M7738 2299L7746 2300"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7642 2226h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0M7746 2300h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7738 2299h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7738 2299h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Medan. <a href="/atlas?year=1789&amp;focus=deli-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
